Z||000000||DecadenceR||05-31-2001||09:23 PM||||Okay. I am taking apart my 4-cyl this weekend to replace the valve seals. While I have it all apart, i'm going to be doing some preventive things as well.<BR>I was thinking about putting in new performance valve springs. The literature with it says they can be used with any cams that have a lift up to .005". <BR>Now, I am not replacing the cam (not going though all that) so will these springs work with my cam? <BR>And would they give me any performance benifit or wouild it be a waste of money?<BR>And any tips you all have would be nore than appreciated, as usual.||||reg||5
Z||000001||88formula||05-31-2001||09:53 PM||||Unless your springs are weak and causing the valves to float than you won't see any performance benefit by switching to stiffer springs especially with a 4500 rpm reline. You may even loose a little power because the stiffer springs are more of a parasitic drag on the motor than the stock springs although the valve train friction only makes up about 7% of the frictional loses in the engine. Stiffer springs are usually installed when the valve lift is increased or the RPM range of the engine is increased via a camshaft. <P>I cheap way to increase your spring tension is to install shims under the springs that you can get at any machine shop. Make sure you check for coil bind at maximum valve lift if you do this. If you want to pump up the performance than you might want to get a used head and have a performance valve job done to it. Than you can switch the heads over on a weekend. You would probably only gain a few horsepower but that would be throughout the whole rpm range. <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
